theory.intro:
A **parabola** meets the axes at its **intercepts** and turns at its **vertex**. The vertical line through the vertex is the **axis of symmetry**. All four features can be read straight off the graph.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[-{Stealth}] (-0.6,0) -- (5.8,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[-{Stealth}] (0,-2.2) -- (0,3.6) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,blue!70!black,domain=0.05:4.95,samples=100,variable=\x,<->] plot ({\x},{0.55*(\x-1)*(\x-4)});
\draw[dashed] (2.5,-1.7) -- (2.5,3.1);
\fill (1,0) circle (2pt);
\fill (4,0) circle (2pt);
\fill (0,2.2) circle (2pt);
\fill (2.5,-1.2375) circle (2pt);
\node[below] at (1,-0.15) {$x$-intercept};
\node[below] at (4,-0.15) {$x$-intercept};
\node[left] at (-0.15,2.2) {$y$-intercept};
\node[right] at (2.7,-1.2375) {vertex};
\node[right] at (2.6,2.8) {axis of symmetry};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

