workedExamples.solution_text:
The curve touches the horizontal axis at $(-1,0)$, so there is one $x$-intercept. It crosses the vertical axis at $(0,-1)$.

The turning point is also $(-1,0)$. The vertical line through it is the axis of symmetry, $x=-1$.

The parabola opens downwards, so the vertex is a maximum. The maximum value is $y=0$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}
\definecolor{curveblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\begin{axis}[width=7cm,height=7cm,axis lines=middle,axis line style={black,<->},xmin=-3.5,xmax=2.5,ymin=-5,ymax=2,xtick={-3,-2,...,2},ytick={-4,-3,...,1},ticklabel style={font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont,text=black},label style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},xlabel={$x$},ylabel={$y$},scaled ticks=false]
\addplot[color=curveblue,thick,<->,domain=-3.1:1.1,samples=100] {-(x+1)*(x+1)};
\draw[black,dashed] (axis cs:-1,-4.5) -- (axis cs:-1,1.5);
\addplot[black,only marks,mark=*] coordinates {(-1,0) (0,-1)};
\node[anchor=south east,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black] at (axis cs:-1,0) {$(-1,0)$};
\node[anchor=north west,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black] at (axis cs:0,-1) {$(0,-1)$};
\draw[black] (axis cs:-1,1.4) -- (axis cs:-1.7,1.4);
\node[anchor=east,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black] at (axis cs:-1.7,1.4) {$x=-1$};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

