workedExamples.solution_text:
Read the turning point: the vertex is $(-1,1)$. The axis of symmetry is the vertical line $x=-1$.

The curve crosses the vertical axis at $(0,2)$.

The parabola opens upwards, so the vertex is a minimum and the minimum value is $y=1$. The whole curve lies above the horizontal axis, so there are no $x$-intercepts.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}
\definecolor{curveblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\begin{axis}[width=7cm,height=7cm,axis lines=middle,axis line style={black,<->},xmin=-4.5,xmax=2.5,ymin=-1,ymax=8,xtick={-4,-3,...,2},ytick={0,1,...,7},ticklabel style={font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont,text=black},label style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},xlabel={$x$},ylabel={$y$},scaled ticks=false]
\addplot[color=curveblue,thick,<->,domain=-3.5:1.5,samples=100] {(x+1)*(x+1)+1};
\draw[black,dashed] (axis cs:-1,0) -- (axis cs:-1,7);
\addplot[black,only marks,mark=*] coordinates {(-1,1) (0,2)};
\node[anchor=north east,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black] at (axis cs:-1,1) {$(-1,1)$};
\node[anchor=south west,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black] at (axis cs:0,2) {$(0,2)$};
\node[anchor=west,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black] at (axis cs:-1,6.5) {$x=-1$};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

